From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dima Nikiforov <vnikiforov@berkeley.edu>
Date: Tue, 16 Sep 2026 00:05:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] mbp: name the custom-0 packed-SIMD ops in the decoded trace

WHAT WAS NOT BROKEN, measured before touching anything. Control-flow
reconstruction already handled MBP correctly. rvdasm does not know opcode 0x0b,
so disassemble_all() inserts a placeholder Insn named "unknown"; the placeholder
takes its length from the raw word's low two bits (0b11 -> 4 bytes) and its
kind_mask from the name (no match in the branch/jump tables -> 0). The four MBP
ops are plain non-branching ALU ops, so "four bytes, not a branch" is exactly
right and step_bb() walks straight through them.

On a 1,320,030-instruction trace of samples/pext_selftest (all four ops, 5296
dynamic executions across 25 static sites), the decoded PC sequence is IDENTICAL
to spike's own tacit.debug ground truth for every instruction, and the Perfetto
output is byte-identical before and after this patch. The 5 unclosed Perfetto
slices at the end of that trace are the frames that were live when tracing
stopped; scripts/10_tacit_hello.sh's golden hello trace has the same 5.

WHAT THIS FIXES is the name and nothing else. trace.txt printed

    0x800003a4: unknown

for all 5296 of them, which in a tutorial about reading traces is a dead end:
the reader cannot tell a packed dot product from a data word that was
disassembled by accident. After this it prints

    0x800003a4: mbp.dot8 x13, x10, x11

Only "unknown" placeholders are rewritten, and only the four exact encodings
(mask 0xfe00707f): funct3 4..7 and every funct7 != 0 stay "unknown", because
the hardware traps those as illegal and naming them would describe something
that cannot execute. CLIP8 prints two operands because the spec says rs2 is
ignored.

The encoding is frozen in fpga/pynq-z2/sw/pext.h in the iiswc-tutorial repo;
see fpga/pynq-z2/docs/PEXT_SPEC.md section 3.0. `cargo test` covers the four
encodings, the two rejection cases and the straight-line classification.
---
diff --git a/src/backend/stack_unwinder.rs b/src/backend/stack_unwinder.rs
index e144e8b..58046b7 100644
--- a/src/backend/stack_unwinder.rs
+++ b/src/backend/stack_unwinder.rs
@@ -72,12 +72,14 @@ impl StackUnwinder {
                 if sh_flags & (SHF_EXECINSTR as u64) != 0 {
                     let addr = section.address();
                     let data = section.data()?;
-                    let sec_map = das.disassemble_all(&data, addr);
+                    let mut sec_map = das.disassemble_all(&data, addr);
+                    let named = crate::mbp::annotate(&mut sec_map);
                     debug!(
-                        "section `{}` @ {:#x}: {} insns",
+                        "section `{}` @ {:#x}: {} insns ({} MBP)",
                         section.name().unwrap_or("<unnamed>"),
                         addr,
-                        sec_map.len()
+                        sec_map.len(),
+                        named
                     );
                     insn_map.extend(sec_map);
                 }
